      "html": "<p>The species attains a maximum length of about 20 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/campylomormyrus_compressirostris_en/",
      "html": "<p>Currently only very occasionally importations from the DR Congo appear – mainly due to the unsatisfying flight situation. Systematics of Campylomormyrus are rather complicated; in all probability these fish belong to the species C. compressirostris which has been regarded as a synonym of C. rhynchophorus for a long time.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/campylomormyrus_compressirostris_en/",
      "html": "<p>Frozen bloodworm are a proven basic food for these fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/campylomormyrus_compressirostris_en/",
      "html": "<p>Against conspecifics these elephant fishes are very quarrelsome. So one must offer a lot of hiding places if more than one specimen is kept in a tank.</p>"
    }
  ],
